<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Lullaby · c. 1907 · Oceania</p><h1 class="sg-title">Hine E Hine</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A lullaby in the Māori language, written around 1907 by Fanny Howie, who sang as Princess Te Rangi Pai.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Fanny Howie (Princess Te Rangi Pai)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Fanny Howie (Princess Te Rangi Pai)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Māori of New Zealand</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Māori</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>New Zealand</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>c. 1907</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Lullaby</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words and music by Fanny Rose Howie, Princess Te Rangi Pai (c. 1907; she died in 1916))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hine-e-hine">Hine E Hine</a>&quot; is a lullaby in Māori written in around 1907 by Fanny Howie, who performed under the stage name Princess Te Rangi Pai. It was published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> by Beal, Stuttard &amp; Co., around 1914, as &quot;A Maori Slumber <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> – Hine E Hine&quot;, under her stage name; a copy is held by the Alexander Turnbull Library of the National Library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>.</p><p>From 1975 to 1994 the television channel TV2 used an instrumental version as its closedown song, played with a cartoon of the Goodnight Kiwi. Singers have kept it in the repertoire since. It opened Kiri Te Kanawa's 1999 album Maori <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a>, Hayley Westenra sang it on her 2003 album Pure, and it appears on the album The José Carreras Collection. The Phoenix Foundation performed it in the 2010 film Boy.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A lullaby, published as a &quot;slumber song&quot;: a song sung to settle a child to sleep. Its mood is quiet and gentle.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Māori</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Māori</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">E tangi ana koe</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You are weeping,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">Hine e hine</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>girl, O girl;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">Kua ngenge ana koe</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you are weary,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">Hine e hine.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>girl, O girl.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">Kāti tō pōuri rā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Cease your sorrow now,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">Noho i te aroha</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>rest in the love,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">Te ngākau o te Matua</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the heart of the Father,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="mi">Hine e hine.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>girl, O girl.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Fanny Rose Howie (c. 1907); as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hine_E_Hine" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (4)</summary><ul><li>lines 1.2, 1.4, 2.4: 'Hine' is a girl or young woman, used as a loving address to a child; 'e' is the vocative particle.</li><li>line 1.3: 'Kua ngenge ana koe' mixes the perfect 'kua' with the continuous 'ana'; the sense is 'you have become weary'.</li><li>lines 2.2-2.3: 'Noho i te aroha / Te ngākau o te Matua' may be read 'stay in the love, the heart of the Father' or 'stay in the love of the Father's heart'; 'te Matua' is the Father (God, or the child's father).</li><li>line 2.1: 'rā' adds emphasis or distance, here 'now, there'.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="Lt8EOWo6x80" data-title="Hine E Hine: St Joseph's Māori Girls' College"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">St Joseph's Māori Girls' College</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel St Joseph's Maori Girls College - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lt8EOWo6x80" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">TV2 closedown song, with the Goodnight Kiwi cartoon</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1975</span><span class="sg-chain-h">instrumental version, used until 1994</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Maori Songs (Kiri Te Kanawa)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1999</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording, the album's opening song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pure (Hayley Westenra)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2003</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The José Carreras Collection (José Carreras)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Boy (film; The Phoenix Foundation)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2010</span><span class="sg-chain-h">performed in the film</span></li></ul></section>
